1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,396
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,396 --> 00:00:09,645
This is chapter fourteen: the language features of Java 21 to 26.

3
00:00:10,095 --> 00:00:16,465
Java 21 and 25 are the long-term releases most teams will run for the rest of the decade.

4
00:00:16,465 --> 00:00:20,830
Virtual threads and pattern matching have their own chapters.

5
00:00:20,830 --> 00:00:30,277
This one covers the smaller changes that remove long-standing papercuts, and one big shift in what a Java source file can look like.

6
00:00:31,177 --> 00:00:34,777
Two different motivations run through these features.

7
00:00:34,777 --> 00:00:41,299
Some remove ceremony: unnamed variables, Markdown doc comments, and flexible constructor bodies.

8
00:00:41,299 --> 00:00:45,171
Those apply everywhere and should become normal practice.

9
00:00:45,621 --> 00:00:51,848
Others lower the on-ramp for learners and scripts: compact source files and module imports.

10
00:00:51,848 --> 00:00:55,885
They're useful there, but mostly wrong in a large codebase.

11
00:00:55,885 --> 00:00:56,843
Let's see why.

12
00:00:57,743 --> 00:01:00,294
Unnamed variables, final in Java 22.

13
00:01:00,294 --> 00:01:06,106
In a record pattern, every component used to need a name, even ones you never use.

14
00:01:06,106 --> 00:01:09,437
Now an underscore says, I don't need this part.

15
00:01:09,437 --> 00:01:14,895
The pattern still checks the component count, so it's not ignoring the shape.

16
00:01:15,345 --> 00:01:16,960
It works elsewhere too.

17
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:20,191
A catch where only the exception type matters.

18
00:01:20,191 --> 00:01:23,001
A resource acquired for its side effect.

19
00:01:23,001 --> 00:01:26,162
A lambda that only uses its second parameter.

20
00:01:26,162 --> 00:01:31,008
The old convention of naming things ignored was invisible to tooling.

21
00:01:31,008 --> 00:01:33,115
Underscore tells the compiler.

22
00:01:34,015 --> 00:01:37,437
Flexible constructor bodies, final in Java 25.

23
00:01:37,437 --> 00:01:41,528
Until now, nothing could come before the call to super.

24
00:01:41,528 --> 00:01:50,380
So validating an argument meant a private static helper, called inside the super arguments, purely to satisfy the rule.

25
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:53,727
Now you just validate first, then call super.

26
00:01:54,177 --> 00:01:57,626
There are limits, and they're why the rule existed.

27
00:01:57,626 --> 00:02:03,306
A superclass constructor can call an overridden method before the subclass is ready.

28
00:02:03,306 --> 00:02:12,908
So the prologue can validate, compute values, and assign the class's own fields, but it can't read fields, call instance methods, or use this.

29
00:02:13,808 --> 00:02:17,196
Markdown doc comments, final in Java 23.

30
00:02:17,196 --> 00:02:24,734
Triple-slash comments use Markdown instead of HTML: paragraphs, code fences, even tables.

31
00:02:24,734 --> 00:02:27,952
Javadoc tags like at param still work.

32
00:02:28,402 --> 00:02:36,591
Compare that with the old HTML tags, which is why most Java documentation is one unformatted paragraph.

33
00:02:36,591 --> 00:02:41,282
The two styles coexist, so convert files as you touch them.

34
00:02:41,282 --> 00:02:43,826
It's not worth a dedicated pass.

35
00:02:44,726 --> 00:02:47,876
Compact source files, final in Java 25.

36
00:02:47,876 --> 00:02:54,660
No class declaration, and main doesn't need to be static, public, or take arguments.

37
00:02:54,660 --> 00:03:01,444
java dot base is imported automatically, and IO dot println replaces System dot out.

38
00:03:01,444 --> 00:03:05,078
Run it directly with java Reconcile dot java.

39
00:03:05,528 --> 00:03:15,447
That's genuinely useful for operational scripts, like a reconciliation check or a one-off data fix, where you'd otherwise write a shell script.

40
00:03:15,447 --> 00:03:18,499
It's ordinary Java, with types and your IDE.

41
00:03:18,949 --> 00:03:21,261
But it's not for application code.

42
00:03:21,261 --> 00:03:30,511
Everything lives in the unnamed package, so nothing else can import it, and as soon as it needs a test it becomes a normal class anyway.

43
00:03:31,411 --> 00:03:35,509
Module import declarations, also final in Java 25.

44
00:03:35,509 --> 00:03:39,443
One line imports every package a module exports.

45
00:03:39,443 --> 00:03:41,246
The risk is ambiguity.

46
00:03:41,246 --> 00:03:47,557
Import java dot base and java dot sql, and the simple name Date is ambiguous.

47
00:03:47,557 --> 00:03:52,638
A single-type import resolves it, because it takes precedence.

48
00:03:53,088 --> 00:03:54,305
Here's the catch.

49
00:03:54,305 --> 00:03:58,027
That ambiguity only shows up where the name is used.

50
00:03:58,027 --> 00:04:04,899
So a future JDK that adds a class to an exported package can break code that compiled yesterday.

51
00:04:04,899 --> 00:04:08,335
In a long-lived codebase, keep explicit imports.

52
00:04:08,335 --> 00:04:12,629
Tooling maintains them, and they document your dependencies.

53
00:04:13,529 --> 00:04:14,744
A few internals.

54
00:04:14,744 --> 00:04:17,857
The underscore change took fifteen years.

55
00:04:17,857 --> 00:04:22,488
Java 9 made a lone underscore a compile error, to reserve it.

56
00:04:22,488 --> 00:04:29,017
So by the time Java 22 gave it a meaning, no working code could be using it as a name.

57
00:04:29,467 --> 00:04:34,318
The rule about super was always a language rule, not a JVM rule.

58
00:04:34,318 --> 00:04:37,275
Bytecode could always run before super.

59
00:04:37,275 --> 00:04:43,035
Java 25 relaxed the language rule while keeping the safety it was there for.

60
00:04:43,485 --> 00:04:49,141
And a compact source file compiles to a real, final class in the unnamed package.

61
00:04:49,141 --> 00:04:56,541
The launcher creates an instance and calls main on it, which is why instance fields and methods just work.

62
00:04:57,441 --> 00:04:58,656
Production rules.

63
00:04:58,656 --> 00:05:03,586
Use underscore for unused components, catch parameters and resources.

64
00:05:03,586 --> 00:05:08,088
Validate in the constructor prologue instead of static helpers.

65
00:05:08,088 --> 00:05:11,517
Write Markdown javadoc for new and touched docs.

66
00:05:11,517 --> 00:05:15,019
Use compact source files for operational scripts.

67
00:05:15,019 --> 00:05:18,305
And keep explicit imports in application code.

68
00:05:18,755 --> 00:05:24,997
Never use compact source files for application code, or module imports in a long-lived codebase.

69
00:05:24,997 --> 00:05:33,318
Don't assume the prologue makes constructors safe, since calling overridable methods from a constructor is still a design error.

70
00:05:33,318 --> 00:05:36,959
Don't use underscore where a name would help the reader.

71
00:05:36,959 --> 00:05:40,600
And don't use primitive type patterns in production yet.

72
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:42,030
They're still preview.

73
00:05:42,930 --> 00:05:51,783
Java 21, a long-term release, brought virtual threads, pattern matching for switch, record patterns and sequenced collections.

74
00:05:52,233 --> 00:05:55,732
Java 22 finalised unnamed variables.

75
00:05:56,182 --> 00:06:01,583
Java 23 finalised Markdown doc comments, and dropped string templates.

76
00:06:01,583 --> 00:06:03,435
That's the next chapter.

77
00:06:03,885 --> 00:06:13,272
Java 25, the current long-term release, brought flexible constructors, module imports, compact source files, and scoped values.

78
00:06:13,722 --> 00:06:21,097
And Java 26 continues previews of structured concurrency, primitive patterns and lazy constants.

79
00:06:21,097 --> 00:06:27,013
Preview features need enable-preview, so check their status against your JDK.

80
00:06:27,913 --> 00:06:28,573
To recap.

81
00:06:28,573 --> 00:06:32,460
Underscore tells the compiler a binding isn't needed.

82
00:06:32,460 --> 00:06:38,255
Constructors can now validate before calling super, but still can't touch this.

83
00:06:38,255 --> 00:06:40,676
Adopt Markdown javadoc as you go.

84
00:06:40,676 --> 00:06:44,343
Compact source files replace scripts, not classes.

85
00:06:44,343 --> 00:06:49,331
And module imports are fine in scripts but risky in long-lived code.

86
00:06:49,781 --> 00:06:56,719
Next is chapter fifteen, a post-mortem on string templates: the feature that was previewed, then withdrawn.

87
00:06:56,719 --> 00:06:58,016
Thanks for watching.
